WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.120
A DFIR toolkit is not finished when the first version is assembled.

00:00:04.340 --> 00:00:13.620
Tools change, operating systems change, cloud services change, log formats change, licenses change, and attacker behavior changes.

00:00:13.840 --> 00:00:15.760
Maintenance keeps the toolkit useful.

00:00:15.980 --> 00:00:27.180
Without maintenance, responders may discover during an incident that a tool no longer runs, a parser no longer understands the current log format, or a download link points to an untrusted source.

00:00:27.400 --> 00:00:29.400
Track tool versions and licensing.

00:00:29.620 --> 00:00:35.540
Different versions may parse artifacts differently, support different operating systems, or produce different output.

00:00:35.760 --> 00:00:41.760
Licensing can affect where a tool may be installed, who may use it, and whether it can be included in offline media.

00:00:41.980 --> 00:00:47.500
Trusted downloads matter because DFIR tools often run with high privileges and handle sensitive evidence.

00:00:47.720 --> 00:00:53.240
The toolkit should include source records, hashes when available, and a process for validating updates.

00:00:53.460 --> 00:00:58.740
Offline copies are useful when networks are unavailable, restricted, or untrusted.

00:00:58.960 --> 00:01:01.840
They should not become stale archives that are never reviewed.

00:01:02.060 --> 00:01:05.900
Keep offline media organized, labeled, and refreshed.

00:01:06.120 --> 00:01:12.680
Include documentation, procedures, forms, known dependencies, and approved installers where appropriate.

00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:18.660
Retire obsolete tools so responders do not pick unsafe or unsupported options during stress.

00:01:18.880 --> 00:01:23.200
Lab validation is the maintenance habit that makes the toolkit real.

00:01:23.420 --> 00:01:33.820
Test collection procedures against sample systems, validate parsers with known artifacts, rehearse evidence storage and transfer, and confirm that analysts can follow the documentation.

00:01:34.040 --> 00:01:36.760
Readiness checks should be scheduled, not improvised.

00:01:36.980 --> 00:01:43.140
A maintained toolkit gives the team confidence because it has already been exercised before the incident arrives.
